/-!
# Finite disk adjunctions

The spaces below are actual quotients, with Mathlib's quotient topology.
Their cells are the original Euclidean disks of `TopCat.disk`; no Hausdorff
or CW property is included in the input. The tower is indexed by its
constructed endpoint so each attaching map lands in the actual preceding
quotient. Arbitrary continuous boundary maps, including self-identifications,
are permitted.

Source: Hatcher, *Algebraic Topology*, Appendix pp.519–522, particularly
the inductive adjunction construction, characteristic-map topology and
subcomplex topology on pp.519–520, and Proposition A.3's radial-neighborhood
proof on p.522. DAG: `topo.hatcher_finite_cell_adjunction_hausdorff_g2`.
-/

noncomputable section

open Set TopCat _root_.Topology
open scoped TopCat

universe u

namespace HolomorphicAnosov.FiniteCellAdjunction

/-- Every finite iterated disk adjunction is a compact Hausdorff finite CW
complex, with its actual characteristic-map topology and closed subcomplexes.

The imported disk adjunction, tower, cell and skeleton definitions construct
the carrier and topology directly, rather than choosing data subject to the
desired conclusion. Native `TopCat.CWComplex` uses
Euclidean disks, as does this construction. The explicit finite cell set,
disjointness, lower-boundary and characteristic topology clauses identify
the cells with the original attached disks, independently of a choice of
native CW witness. An embedding of an open disk into its image is precisely
a homeomorphism onto that cell. The final subcomplex clause uses the induced
subtype topology and additionally states its characteristic-map closed-set
test; no topology is silently replaced.

Natural-language proof (Hatcher Appendix pp.519–522): At one adjunction,
old points remain distinct, and no interior disk point participates in the
boundary relation. Each open disk is an open saturated subset of the
disjoint union, so its quotient restriction is a homeomorphism onto its
cell. An open set in the old skeleton extends through each boundary by
the radial collar `1/2 < r ≤ 1` in directions whose attaching image is in
that open set. The resulting saturated open set restricts to precisely
the original open set. Thus the skeleton has its given induced topology.
Its complement is the union of new open cells, hence open, so the skeleton
is closed. Composing these inclusions proves the claim for every stage.

The quotient is also the categorical attaching pushout used by
`HomotopicalAlgebra.AttachCells`. Take the boundary coproduct to be
`Fin k × ∂𝔻 n` and the disk coproduct to be `Fin k × 𝔻 n`, with their
disjoint-union topologies. Their maps to the old space and disk coproduct
are `(i,z) ↦ a i z` and `(i,z) ↦ (i,diskBoundaryInclusion n z)`.
The quotient projections commute on each boundary generator. Given
continuous maps from the old space and from all disks which agree on these
generators, their disjoint-union function is continuous, respects the
generating relation, hence its equivalence closure, and descends by
`Quotient.lift` for the setoid generated by the boundary relation.
Its descended function is continuous by the coinduced quotient topology. Surjectivity of the
quotient projection proves uniqueness. These are the pushout universal
property and the coproduct universal properties, with exactly the original
quotient topology, not a separately selected pushout topology.

The quotient closed-set test inductively says that a set is closed exactly
when its inverse image under every original characteristic disk map is
closed, including the zero-cells. The original open cells are disjoint,
cover the space, and have boundary in lower-dimensional cells. This is
the inductive CW construction; the final weak topology needs no extra
condition in finite dimension. The total label set is finite because the
initial point set, each attached family, and the number of stages are finite.

For the native `TopCat.CWComplex` witness, its stage zero is the empty
(-1)-skeleton. Attach one zero-disk for each point of the original finite
discrete space `Q0`; every zero-disk is a point and every zero-boundary is
empty, so this coproduct is homeomorphic to `Q0` with its given topology.
Set native stage `n+1` equal to the original `Qn` for `0 ≤ n ≤ d`.
The successor maps from stage `n+1` to stage `n+2` are the original
attachments of `(n+1)`-disks. The pushout property just proved supplies
their `AttachCells` data. After native stage `d+1`, use the final space
constantly, identity transition maps, and empty attachment families in
each higher dimension. The maps from stages into the final space form a
cocone. A compatible cocone is determined uniquely by its map at stage
`d+1`, so this is the colimit cocone. There are no positive limit stages
in the natural-number indexing; the initial identification and successor
pushouts therefore give precisely the required `RelativeCellComplex`
data for the initial map to the final space. This constructs the native
CW existence clause directly, without invoking any unproved equivalence
between classical and categorical CW definitions. The exported original
cell properties concern `Cell T` and its literal characteristic maps;
the existential native witness has no additional exported identification.

For separation follow the written proof of Proposition A.3, without
assuming Hausdorffness in advance. Singletons pull back to closed sets
under the characteristic maps, and so are closed. For disjoint closed
sets `A,B`, inductively construct neighborhoods using interior epsilon
neighborhoods of their disk preimages and radial collars above the
already disjoint lower-skeleton neighborhoods. The two closed disk
preimages are compact. If their distance had no positive lower bound,
compact subsequences would produce a common point. A collar direction
set need not be compact: if directions above the A-neighborhood approached
the compact B-preimage, a subsequential limit would lie on the sphere
above the open B-neighborhood, contradicting disjointness of the two
direction sets. The same argument applies with A and B exchanged.
Choose each collar width less than one half and one third of all applicable
positive gaps. Interior neighborhoods, opposite collar/interior pairs,
and the two collars are then disjoint. Finite induction gives disjoint
open neighborhoods of A and B, proving normality and Hausdorffness.

Each characteristic disk is compact in its finite-dimensional Euclidean
model. Its continuous image is compact, as is the finite discrete initial
space. Their finite union covers the final space, which is therefore
compact. This proves compactness without using separation of the target.

For a cell selection closed under characteristic boundary images, its
union has its own induced attaching construction. Inductively, the
preimage of the union in an unselected disk lies on the boundary and is
closed there; selected disks lie entirely in the union. The characteristic
test makes the union closed. If a subset is closed in the selected
attaching topology, its preimage in an unselected disk is the closed
boundary attaching preimage of its lower-skeleton part, while selected
disk preimages are closed by hypothesis. The full characteristic test
therefore makes it closed in the ambient space. Conversely ambient
closedness restricts continuously. This proves the induced topology and
the claimed CW structure on the subcomplex. Empty initial spaces, empty
attachment families, noninjective boundaries and a zero-stage tower are
included throughout.

Apply the same pushout construction to the selected cells. Boundary
closure means each selected attaching map lands in the selected preceding
stage; the induced-topology argument identifies the resulting selected
quotient with the subtype at that stage. Its finite discrete zero-stage,
successor selected attachments, and constant empty-attachment tail give
the native CW witness on the unchanged subtype `L`.

Stage2 only: this single named theorem is intentionally admitted.
-/
theorem compact_hausdorff_cw {d : ℕ} {X : TopCat.{u}} (T : Tower d X) :
    CompactSpace X ∧ T2Space X ∧ NormalSpace X ∧
    Nonempty (TopCat.CWComplex X) ∧ Finite (Σ n, Cell T n) ∧
    (∀ {m : ℕ} {Y : TopCat.{u}} (S : Stage T m Y), IsClosedEmbedding (stageInclusion S)) ∧
    (∀ (n : ℕ) (c : Cell T n), Continuous (characteristic c) ∧
      IsEmbedding (fun z : 𝔹 n ↦ characteristic c (ballInclusion n z))) ∧
    (Set.univ : Set (Σ n, Cell T n)).PairwiseDisjoint
      (fun c ↦ openCell c.2) ∧
    (⋃ (n : ℕ) (c : Cell T n), openCell c) = Set.univ ∧
    (∀ (n : ℕ) (c : Cell T n),
      boundaryImage c ⊆ ⋃ (m : ℕ) (_ : m < n) (b : Cell T m), openCell b) ∧
    (∀ A : Set X, IsClosed A ↔
      ∀ (n : ℕ) (c : Cell T n), IsClosed (characteristic c ⁻¹' A)) ∧
    (∀ S : (n : ℕ) → Set (Cell T n),
      let L : Set X := ⋃ (n : ℕ) (c : S n), openCell c.val
      (∀ (n : ℕ) (c : Cell T n), c ∈ S n → boundaryImage c ⊆ L) →
      IsClosed L ∧ Nonempty (TopCat.CWComplex (TopCat.of L)) ∧
      (∀ A : Set X, A ⊆ L →
        (IsClosed (Subtype.val ⁻¹' A : Set L) ↔
          ∀ (n : ℕ) (c : Cell T n), c ∈ S n →
            IsClosed (characteristic c ⁻¹' A)))) := by
  refine ⟨tower_compactSpace T, tower_t2Space T, tower_normalSpace T,
    tower_native_cw T, tower_finite_cells T, ?_, ?_, tower_openCells_pairwiseDisjoint T,
    tower_openCell_cover T, ?_, tower_isClosed_iff T, ?_⟩
  · intro m Y S
    exact attachment_stage_isClosedEmbedding S
  · intro n c
    exact ⟨continuous_characteristic c, characteristic_openCell_isEmbedding c⟩
  · intro n c
    exact boundaryImage_subset_lower_openCells c
  · intro S L hboundary
    have htop := tower_selected_topology T S hboundary
    exact ⟨htop.1, tower_selected_native_cw T S hboundary, htop.2⟩

end HolomorphicAnosov.FiniteCellAdjunction
